The heal loop for libpeer (extern/libpeer): observable counters for
every link of the "ask for a key frame -> key frame arrives -> picture
is whole again" chain, across all three repair channels.

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place.  This patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it
is the thirteenth patch of that series; it stacks on top of
0012-audio-observation).

Why
---

A lost packet breaks the H.264 reference chain and the picture smears.
Three channels can repair it, and until now none of them had a CLOSED
loop -- we could see that we asked, never whether the ask was answered:

  * RTCP PLI (engine side, the 0011/B2 contract): pli_sent was counted,
    but "the key frame arrived because we asked" was indistinguishable
    from "it arrived anyway";
  * NACK (engine side): nack_requests / nack_packets_requested were
    counted, but a retransmission that landed was not (reordered_packets
    is the approximate landing counter -- an out-of-order packet that
    filled its slot);
  * 20032 (app side): out of libpeer's scope; correlate its send times
    against idrDelivered growth in this patch's line.

One more gap the device data could not close: keyframes_received (0011)
counts key frames at NAL assembly, so a key frame that arrives inside a
DAMAGED access unit still counts -- correct for "the cloud sent one",
useless for "the picture got better".  Nothing counted key frames that
actually SURVIVED into a delivered access unit.

What
---

  * rtp.c / rtp.h: au_had_idr (per-access-unit transient flag, set in
    the same branches where 0011 counts keyframes_received, cleared by
    rtp_decoder_reset_access_unit) and keyframes_delivered (incremented
    in rtp_decoder_flush_access_unit's success branch).  The gap between
    keyframes_received and keyframes_delivered is OUR loss, not the
    cloud's.

  * peer_connection.c: peer_connection_account_heal_keyframe() runs
    every loop tick.  A key frame that arrives while a PLI is "open"
    (sent at most PEER_HEAL_ANSWER_WINDOW_MS = 3000 ms ago) counts as
    answered -- attribution is by time window, which is the strongest
    attribution an RTCP PLI allows (it carries no packet identity).
    heal_healed_ms records when a DELIVERED key frame last landed.

  * One diagnostic line every 2 s (prefix heal_state -- needs an engine
    whitelist key, see the delivery note):

      heal_state pliSent=.. pliAnswered=.. idrSeen=.. idrDelivered=..
                 nackReq=.. nackPkts=.. recovered=.. auOk=.. auDrop=..
                 healedAgoMs=..

How the criterion changes (AGENTS.md 6.2, rule 8)
---------------------------------------------------

  * the cloud does not answer RTCP PLI: pliSent grows, pliAnswered
    stays 0 (and given the official browser session seems to use no
    RTCP at all, this channel may be structurally dead -- then 20032 is
    the only live request path);
  * the cloud answers but we lose the frame: idrSeen grows,
    idrDelivered does not -- OUR reassembler dropped it;
  * the frame survives but the picture still smears: idrDelivered grows
    while auDrop keeps growing -- the damage is ahead of the repair;
  * healthy: healedAgoMs stays small, auOk grows.

Every one of them moves, so the line is a criterion and not a
decoration.

A host-side self test of the rule itself is next to this file:
tools/libpeer-switch/0013-heal-loop-check.mjs

Note for the Switch build
-------------------------

struct RtpDecoder lives in the installed src/rtp.h and gains two fields
at its END (after audio_opus_packets) -- same rule as 0011/0012: libpeer
must be rebuilt and the installed headers refreshed together with the
engine, otherwise the two sides disagree about the struct layout.

